Vulkan pipelines are created with the device's one VkPipelineCache, which is loaded from CallbackI::cacheRead
at init and handed to CallbackI::cacheWrite as one entry (id "STORMVKP") once no pipeline has been created for
60 frames, at the latest 600 frames after the last save, and at shutdown. Upstream gave every pipeline a cache
of its own (filled from cacheRead by the pipeline's hash) and only merged it into the device's cache afterwards,
so the device's cache was never used to create anything: a pipeline that differs from an earlier one only in
its render state compiled both shaders again, within a run and in every later run, and each entry on disk
repeated the shaders (Adreno 830: 132 entries, 8.3 MB). bgfx::setVkPipelineCreated reports every
vkCreateGraphicsPipelines with the shaders' names (kept from bgfx::setName), the state, its duration, how it
was created and its recipe, so the engine can log the slow ones and create the same pipelines ahead of time.

Pipeline threads (bgfx::setVkPipelineThreads before init: 1..4 of them, niced on Linux/Android) create
pipelines off the render thread, from one queue: first the pipelines of draws that wait, then prewarms.

A draw submitted with the state bit STORM_STATE_PIPELINE_MAY_WAIT (a bit bgfx does not use) whose pipeline does
not exist yet is left out of the frame (counted: bgfx::getVkDrawsLeftOut, bgfx::getVkLastWaitingFrame): its
pipeline goes to the front of the queue, and the draw appears in the first frame that has it. Where the device has
Vulkan 1.3's pipelineCreationCacheControl (enabled then; bgfx::getVkPipelineCacheControl), such a draw first asks
the pipeline cache alone (VK_PIPELINE_CREATE_FAIL_ON_PIPELINE_COMPILE_REQUIRED_BIT) on the render thread: a cached
pipeline is created at once and the draw is not left out; only one that needs a compile goes to the threads. Draws without the
bit and every BGFX_FRAME_FLUSH frame (read-backs) create their pipelines on the render thread as before: one
still queued is taken back and created there, one a pipeline thread is creating is waited for, that one only.

A recipe (the arguments of getPipeline, the shaders by hash and the render pass by its formats, as
getRenderPass made it) creates a pipeline again: bgfx::prewarmVkPipeline queues it behind the draws' pipelines,
from the frame in which its program exists; a recipe whose program has other shaders now is reported unusable.
bgfx::getVkPipelinesPending counts what was asked for and is not created yet.

Before a shader or program is destroyed, the queued jobs that use it are dropped and the ones being created are
waited for; at shutdown and on a wireframe switch, all of them.

